Race-condition protection is missing here. The detailSeqRef pattern is applied to the history-switch path (with explicit seq bump + guard), but reload() — called on mount (taskId mode) and after rollback via onRolledBack — does not bump or check the token. In taskId mode with multiple runs, if the user clicks a history button while the initial traceDetail() is still in flight, the late-arriving initial response can clobber the freshly selected detail and re-flip selection. Bump detailSeqRef.current at the start of reload() and guard the .then(async) branches the same way the history-button handler does.

```ts
const reload = (pickId: number | null) => {
  setLoading(true);
  setError(null);
  const seq = ++detailSeqRef.current;
  if (traceId != null) {
    traceDetail(traceId).then((d) => {
      if (seq !== detailSeqRef.current) return;
      ...
    }).catch((e) => { if (seq !== detailSeqRef.current) return; setError(String(e)); })
      .finally(() => { if (seq === detailSeqRef.current) setLoading(false); });
    return;
  }
  traceListByTask(taskId ?? "").then(async (list) => {
    if (seq !== detailSeqRef.current) return;
    ...
    if (pick) { const d = await traceDetail(pick.id); if (seq !== detailSeqRef.current) return; setDetail(d); }
  })...
};
```

The comment block above the ref explains the intent — this fix makes reload() actually honor it.

